#!/bin/busybox sh
# The console's login shell. Sources /etc/profile, then becomes the shell.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, WHEN `getty -l /bin/sh` LOOKS LIKE IT WOULD DO.
# It does not, and the difference was measured on the real kernel:
#
#   PATH=[/sbin:/usr/sbin:/bin:/usr/bin]     <- busybox's built-in default
#   PS1=[\w \$ ]                             <- busybox's built-in default
#   argv0=[/bin/sh]
#
# A shell sources /etc/profile only when it is a LOGIN shell, and a shell is a
# login shell only when its argv[0] begins with `-`. busybox getty execs the
# program named by -l with argv[0] set to the path it was given, so `/bin/sh`
# starts as a plain interactive shell and /etc/profile is never read. The file
# was shipped, was correct, and had no reader.
#
# AND THE DEFAULT IS NOT MERELY COSMETIC. busybox's PATH puts /sbin and /bin
# FIRST; /etc/profile puts /usr/bin first, deliberately, because that is the
# build's PATH. Reversed, every busybox applet in /bin shadows the real binary
# in /usr/bin -- so the booted system would resolve busybox's `sh`, `ls` and
# `grep` in preference to the packages this project spent sixty-two builds
# producing, and `veron compare` would be describing files nothing runs.
#
# WHY NOT `exec -a -sh /bin/sh`. That is a bashism; busybox ash does not
# accept it, and a login path that depends on which shell implements which
# extension is the sort of thing that works until the shell is swapped. A
# script that sources the file itself needs no argv[0] trick and works under
# any POSIX shell.
# THE HOME INIT HANDS DOWN IS ROOT'S, AND IT REACHED THE VERIFIER. This
# shell inherits PID 1's environment, which carries HOME=/root -- set,
# non-empty, and wrong for the veron user this getty runs as. Measured on
# hardware: `veron-enroll check` here printed `confdir: /root/.config/veron`
# beside `getpwuid(1000): /home/veron` and refused every factor, while the
# same binary passed the moment HOME was overridden. The verifier now asks
# the passwd entry first and cannot be misled -- but every OTHER program a
# person runs from this console reads HOME the ordinary way, so the value
# is corrected at the door rather than left for each of them to survive.
# From /etc/passwd, the same place getpwuid answers from, with busybox awk.
HOME=$(awk -F: -v u="$(id -u)" '$3==u{print $6}' /etc/passwd)
[ -n "$HOME" ] && export HOME || export HOME=/home/veron

[ -r /etc/profile ] && . /etc/profile
exec /bin/busybox sh -i
